Neon spaces (M3), part 1 of 4: sidebar pager.

The tab list of the vertical tab strip becomes a pager with one page per
space. At rest it shows the window's own tab strip view, the real page,
under the space's name. While pages move, the neighbouring spaces are shown
by peek pages: second and third tab strips built on the same tab strip model
(their own RootTabCollectionNode, controller and drag handler), so both
lists stay live. A switch by command slides the pages with the tab strip's
200 ms timing, activates the target space as it starts, and lets the real
and peek pages trade places in one frame. Each space keeps its scroll
position. With one space, outside the vertical layout or outside a normal
window, the tab strip looks and behaves as before.

Hooks: BaseTabStripRegionView wraps its tab strip view in the pager and
unwraps it before removal; RootTabCollectionNode::Init can observe the model
without becoming its tab strip UI; TabView asks which tab a page draws as
active; the spaces filter gains per-page spaces, and the window controller a
presenter that the pager implements. Neon code, GPL-3.0.

M1b: the hidden-tab checks of the tab strip views read a group's or split's
first tab through its child nodes. A split's own collection is destroyed before
its tab views move out, and reading it crashed the browser when a split was
removed, for instance when a window with a split closed.

